 ## check.sh
 
-### §check-fails-open — the check never blocks a commit because of its own failure
+### §check-fails-closed — any failing command aborts the check with a non-zero exit
+- Since: 2026-10-23
+- Decision: `set -euo pipefail`. A failing command aborts the check with a non-zero exit status. The script still ends in `exit 0` when nothing fails.
+- Overrides: §check-fails-open. The user (maintainer) explicitly named that decision and overrode it on 2026-10-23.
+- Reason: the maintainer wants internal failures to surface instead of being silently swallowed.
+- Known trade-off: the failure recorded under §check-fails-open can recur. A `wc` failure on a staged-then-deleted file aborts the check and, if it runs as a pre-commit hook, blocks commits (as on 2026-09-10).
+
+### §check-fails-open — SUPERSEDED by §check-fails-closed (2026-10-23)
 - Since: 2026-09-11
 - Decision: `set -uo pipefail` without `-e`. Every internal error still ends in `exit 0`; the script only warns.
 - Forbidden: `set -e` or `set -o errexit`, and any exit status other than 0.
 - Reason: with `set -e`, a `wc` failure on a staged-then-deleted file aborted the check and blocked every commit in
   the repository for a day (2026-09-10). The check is advisory by design.
+- Superseded: the user overrode this decision on 2026-10-23.
